ne \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nAll other slopes are *sunny*.\n\nCompute the slope of the line through each unordered pair of points of \\(S\\). The results are:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{pair} & \\text{slope}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)-(1,2) & \\infty\\\\\n(1,1)-(1,3) & \\infty\\\\\n(1,1)-(2,1) & 0\\\\\n(1,1)-(2,2) & 1\\\\\n(1,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n(1,2)-(1,3) & \\infty\\\\\n(1,2)-(2,1) & -1\\\\\n(1,2)-(2,2) & 0\\\\\n(1,2)-(3,1) & -\\tfrac12\\\\\n(1,3)-(2,1) & -2\\\\\n(1,3)-(2,2) & -1\\\\\n(1,3)-(3,1) & -1\\\\\n(2,1)-(2,2) & \\infty\\\\\n(2,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n(2,2)-(3,1) & -1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus the set of all slopes that occur is  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,\\infty,-1,1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the **sunny** slopes are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1,\\;-\\tfrac12,\\;-2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Necessity of at\u2011least\u2011two\u2011point lines\n\nWe have three distinct lines that must contain the six points of \\(S\\).  \nIf a line contained only a single point of \\(S\\), the remaining five points would have to be covered by the two remaining lines, which is impossible because a line can contain at most three points of \\(S\\) (no four points of \\(S\\) are collinear).  \nHence **each of the three lines contains at least two points of \\(S\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Possible sunny lines\n\nFrom (3) we see that a sunny line can only be one of the following three lines, each determined uniquely by the two points it must contain:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_{1}&:\\; y=x \\quad\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2) &&(\\text{slope }1),\\\\\n\\ell_{2}&:\\; \\text{through }(1,2),(3,1) &&(\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12),\\\\\n\\ell_{3}&:\\; \\text{through }(1,3),(2,1) &&(\\text{slope }-2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nNo other pair of points of \\(S\\) yields a sunny slope, so any sunny line in a covering must be one of \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of exactly two sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering of \\(S\\) by three distinct lines exists with **exactly two** sunny lines.  \nLet those sunny lines be \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\). By (4) each of them must be one of \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\).\n\nWe examine all possible unordered pairs \\(\\{L_{1},L_{2}\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Case A: \\(\\{L_{1},L_{2}\\}=\\{\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\}\\).\n\n\\(\\ell_{1}\\) covers \\((1,1),(2,2)\\); \\(\\ell_{2}\\) covers \\((1,2),(3,1)\\).  \nThe two points of \\(S\\) not yet covered are \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \nThe line through \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) has slope \\(-2\\) (see the table), which is a **sunny** slope.  \nHence the third line would also be sunny, contradicting the assumption of exactly two sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### Case B: \\(\\{L_{1},L_{2}\\}=\\{\\ell_{1},\\ell_{3}\\}\\).\n\n\\(\\ell_{1}\\) covers \\((1,1),(2,2)\\); \\(\\ell_{3}\\) covers \\((1,3),(2,1)\\).  \nThe uncovered points are \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\); their connecting line has slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) (sunny).  \nAgain the third line would be sunny \u2013 contradiction.\n\n---\n\n#### Case C: \\(\\{L_{1},L_{2}\\}=\\{\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\}\\).\n\n\\(\\ell_{2}\\) covers \\((1,2),(3,1)\\); \\(\\ell_{3}\\) covers \\((1,3),(2,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\); the line through them has slope \\(1\\) (sunny).  \nThus the third line would be sunny, contradiction.\n\n---\n\nThese three cases exhaust all possibilities for two sunny lines, because any sunny line must be one of \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\). In each case the uncovered pair of points determines a line of sunny slope, forcing the third line to be sunny as well.\n\nTherefore a covering of the six points of \\(S\\) by three distinct lines cannot contain exactly two sunny lines.\n\n----------------------------------------