            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non‑sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three\ndirections  \n\n\\[\nx=\\text{const},\\qquad y=\\text{const},\\qquad x+y=\\text{const},\n\\]\n\nand **sunny** otherwise.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Two extreme constructions\n\n*All lines non‑sunny.*  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{D_c\\}_{c=2}^{n+1}\\) are pairwise distinct, none of them\nis sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one of them (its sum\n\\(a+b\\) equals the corresponding \\(c\\)).  Hence the case \\(k=0\\) is\nrealised.\n\n*All lines sunny.*  \nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,x+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i-1}{i}\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\);\nconsequently each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  For a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb-1 = \\frac{i-1}{i}\\,a \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\ni = \\frac{b-1}{a}+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a,b\\) are positive integers with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the right–hand\nside is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) belongs to\n\\(L_i\\) for that integer \\(i\\).  Thus the \\(n\\) sunny lines (6) cover all\npoints of \\(S_n\\), giving the case \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  General construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall produce \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that\ncover \\(S_n\\).\n\n#### 2.1  The non‑sunny part\n\nKeep the **large** diagonals  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_{\\text{large}}=\n\\bigl\\{\\,D_c\\mid c=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(n-k\\) such lines, all non‑sunny.  Every point of a diagonal\n\\(D_c\\) with \\(c\\ge k+2\\) is already covered.\n\n#### 2.2  Points that still have to be covered\n\nThe remaining points lie on the **small** diagonals  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_{\\text{small}}=\n\\bigl\\{\\,D_c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor a diagonal \\(D_c\\) the number of lattice points it contains equals\n\\(c-1\\).  Hence the total number of uncovered points is  \n\n\\[\nN_{\\text{small}}=\\sum_{c=2}^{k+1}(c-1)=1+2+\\dots +k\n                 =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.3  Capacity of sunny lines\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any diagonal, therefore it meets each\ndiagonal of (8) in **at most one** point.  Consequently a single sunny\nline can contain at most \\(k\\) of the points in (9) (one from each of the\n\\(k\\) small diagonals).  With exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines the total\ncapacity is therefore  \n\n\\[\nC_{\\text{max}} = k\\cdot k = k^{2}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (9) we have  \n\n\\[\nN_{\\text{small}}=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k^{2}=C_{\\text{max}}\n\\qquad\\text{for all }k\\ge 1,\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nand the inequality is trivial for \\(k=0\\).  Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines\nare *a priori* sufficient to accommodate all points of (8).\n\n#### 2.4  Realising the assignment (Hall’s theorem)\n\nConsider the bipartite graph \\(G\\) defined as follows.\n\n* Left part \\(U\\): the set of points of (8) (there are \\(N_{\\text{small}}\\) of\n  them).\n* Right part \\(V\\): the set of \\(k\\) sunny lines we are going to create.\n* An edge joins a point \\(p\\in U\\) to a line \\(v\\in V\\) iff we are allowed\n  to place \\(p\\) on \\(v\\); i.e. we require that no line receives two\n  points from the same diagonal.  In the language of the graph this means\n  that each line \\(v\\) may be incident to at most one vertex from each\n  diagonal of \\(\\mathcal D_{\\text{small}}\\).\n\nFor a subset \\(X\\subseteq U\\) let \\(\\mathcal D_X\\) be the set of diagonals\nthat contain at least one point of \\(X\\).  Because each diagonal contributes\nat most one point to any line, the neighbourhood of \\(X\\) in \\(V\\) has\nsize at least  \n\n\\[\n|N_G(X)|\\ge \\frac{|X|}{|\\mathcal D_X|}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nSince each diagonal of \\(\\mathcal D_{\\text{small}}\\) contains at most\n\\(k\\) points, we have \\(|X|\\le k\\,|\\mathcal D_X|\\).  Substituting into\n(12) yields  \n\n\\[\n|N_G(X)|\\ge \\frac{|X|}{|\\mathcal D_X|}\\ge \\frac{k\\,|\\mathcal D_X|}{|\\mathcal D_X|}\n= k .\n\\]\n\nThus every subset of vertices of the left side has a neighbourhood of size at\nleast \\(k\\), which is the total number of vertices on the right.  By **Hall’s\nmarriage theorem** there exists a matching that saturates the whole left\npart \\(U\\).  In other words we can assign each point of (8) to a distinct\nsunny line so that no line receives two points from the same diagonal.\n\n#### 2.5  Defining the sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) let \\(P_j\\) be the (possibly empty) set of points\nassigned to the \\(j\\)-th line by the matching.  By construction, the points\nin \\(P_j\\) lie on distinct diagonals, hence they are not collinear with a\ndirection parallel to any of the forbidden families.  Consequently there is a\nunique line \\(S_j\\) passing through all points of \\(P_j\\); this line is not\nparallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to any diagonal, i.e. it is\nsunny.  Distinct \\(j\\) give distinct lines because the matchings are\ndisjoint.\n\n#### 2.6  The final family of lines\n\nCollect the \\(n-k\\) large diagonals (7) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).  We have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  By construction\n\n* every point of a large diagonal is covered,\n* every point of a small diagonal belongs to the appropriate sunny line,\n* therefore **all** points of \\(S_n\\) are covered.\n\nThe number of sunny lines in the family is precisely \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\),\n\n* a concrete set of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n* exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny,\n* covering the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\nHence every such \\(k\\) is attainable.  Since we are required to use exactly\n\\(n\\) lines, no other value of \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
