or a unique \\(c\\) in the given range, so the covering condition holds. The construction for \\(k=0\\) is valid.\n\n### 4. Extreme construction: all lines sunny  \n\n> \u201cFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n> \\[\n> L_i:\\;y=\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,x+1 .\n> \\tag{6}\n> \\]  \n> The slope \\(\\frac{i-1}{i}\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(L_i\\) is sunny. \u2026  \n> For a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have  \n> \\[\n> b-1 = \\frac{i-1}{i}\\,a \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n> i = \\frac{b-1}{a}+1 .\n> \\]  \n> Since \\(a,b\\) are positive integers with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the right\u2013hand side is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_i\\) for that integer \\(i\\).\u201d\n\n*Critical Error 1 (non\u2011sunny line):* For \\(i=1\\) the slope is \\((1-1)/1=0\\); the line \\(L_1\\) is \\(y=1\\), a horizontal line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, **hence non\u2011sunny**. The claim \u201cdifferent from\u202f0,\u202f\u221e,\u202f\u22121\u201d is false for \\(i=1\\). Therefore the construction does **not** produce \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Critical Error 2 (algebraic mistake):* Solving \\(b-1 = \\frac{i-1}{i}a\\) correctly gives  \n\\[\ni(b-1) = (i-1)a \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; i = \\frac{a}{a-b+1}.\n\\]  \nThe solution\u2019s expression \\(i = \\frac{b-1}{a}+1\\) is algebraically incorrect. Consequently the asserted integer range for \\(i\\) does not follow, and many points \\((a,b)\\) are not on any line \\(L_i\\). Hence the \u201call\u2011sunny\u201d construction fails, and the claim that \\(k=n\\) is attainable is unsupported.\n\n### 5. General construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)  \n\n> \u201cFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). \u2026  \n> Keep the **large** diagonals  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal D_{\\text{large}}=\n> \\bigl\\{\\,D_c\\mid c=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n> \\tag{7}\n> \\]  \n> \u2026  \n> The remaining points lie on the **small** diagonals  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal D_{\\text{small}}=\n> \\bigl\\{\\,D_c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n> \\tag{8}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Correctness of the split:* The large diagonals (7) are indeed non\u2011sunny and cover all points with sum \\(c\\ge k+2\\). The small diagonals (8) contain the uncovered points, exactly those with sum \\(c\\le k+1\\). No issue here.\n\n#### 5.1 Counting uncovered points  \n\n> \u201cFor a diagonal \\(D_c\\) the number of lattice points it contains equals \\(c-1\\). \u2026  \n> \\[\n> N_{\\text{small}}=\\sum_{c=2}^{k+1}(c-1)=1+2+\\dots +k\n>                  =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n> \\tag{9}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Correctness:* Since each diagonal \\(D_c\\) (with \\(c\\ge2\\)) has exactly \\(c-1\\) points, the sum is correct. Hence (9) holds.\n\n#### 5.2 Capacity of sunny lines  \n\n> \u201cA sunny line \u2026 meets each diagonal of (8) in **at most one** point. Consequently a single sunny line can contain at most \\(k\\) of the points in (9) (one from each of the \\(k\\) small diagonals). \u2026  \n> \\[\n> C_{\\text{max}} = k\\cdot k = k^{2}.\n> \\tag{10}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Correctness:* A line not parallel to any diagonal can intersect each diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) at most once, so the bound of at most one point per diagonal is valid. Since there are \\(k\\) small diagonals, a sunny line can contain at most \\(k\\) points from the set (9). Thus the total capacity of \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most \\(k^{2}\\). No error.\n\n#### 5.3 Feasibility inequality  \n\n> \u201cBecause of (9) we have  \n> \\[\n> N_{\\text{small}}=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k^{2}=C_{\\text{max}}\n> \\qquad\\text{for all }k\\ge 1,\n> \\tag{11}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Correctness:* For \\(k\\ge1\\), \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2 \\le k^{2}\\) holds (equivalent to \\(k+1 \\le 2k\\) i.e. \\(k\\ge1\\)). So (11) is correct.\n\n#### 5.4 Attempted Hall\u2011type assignment  \n\n> \u201cConsider the bipartite graph \\(G\\) defined as follows.  \n> * Left part \\(U\\): the set of points of (8).  \n> * Right part \\(V\\): the set of \\(k\\) sunny lines we are going to create.  \n> * An edge joins a point \\(p\\in U\\) to a line \\(v\\in V\\) iff we are allowed to place \\(p\\) on \\(v\\); i.e. we require that no line receives two points from the same diagonal. \u2026  \n> For a subset \\(X\\subseteq U\\) let \\(\\mathcal D_X\\) be the set of diagonals that contain at least one point of \\(X\\). \u2026  \n> Since each diagonal of \\(\\mathcal D_{\\text{small}}\\) contains at most \\(k\\) points, we have \\(|X|\\le k\\,|\\mathcal D_X|\\). Substituting into (12) yields \u2026  \n> Thus every subset of vertices of the left side has a neighbourhood of size at least \\(k\\), which is the total number of vertices on the right. By **Hall\u2019s marriage theorem** there exists a matching that saturates the whole left part \\(U\\).\u201d\n\n*Critical Error 3 (misuse of Hall\u2019s theorem):* Hall\u2019s marriage theorem guarantees a **matching** where each vertex on the right is incident to **at most one** vertex on the left. In the present problem each sunny line (right\u2011hand vertex) must be able to accommodate **up to \\(k\\) points** (one per diagonal). The bipartite graph described does not enforce a capacity of 1 on the right side, so Hall\u2019s theorem does **not** apply. The argument that a matching covering all points exists is therefore invalid.\n\n*Critical Error 4 (incorrect conclusion about the line being sunny):* Even if one could assign each point to a line respecting the \u201cat most one per diagonal\u201d rule, the set of points assigned to a given line need not be collinear on a sunny line. Points on distinct diagonals can be collinear on a horizontal line (e.g., \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) lie on the same line \\(y=1\\), which is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis and thus non\u2011sunny). The solution\u2019s statement \u201cthe points in \\(P_j\\) lie on distinct diagonals, hence they are not collinear with a direction parallel to any of the forbidden families\u201d is **false**. Consequently the existence of a sunny line \\(S_j\\) passing through all points of \\(P_j\\) is not guaranteed.\n\nBecause of these two critical flaws, the entire \u201cgeneral construction for arbitrary \\(k\\)\u201d collapses; the proof that every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable is not established.\n\n### 6. Final conclusion  \n\n> \u201cWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), \u2026 Hence every such \\(k\\) is attainable. \u2026  \n> \\[\n> \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Critical Error:* The preceding constructions for \\(k=n\\) and for general \\(k\\) are invalid (see Critical Errors\u202f1\u20134). Therefore the claim that **all** integers \\(k\\) from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\) are attainable is unsupported. The final answer is not proven.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:** The solution fails at several crucial points: the \u201call\u2011sunny\u201d construction does not produce \\(n\\) sunny lines; the algebraic derivation for point\u2011line incidence is wrong; the Hall\u2011theorem argument misapplies a matching theorem to a problem with vertex capacities; and the claim that any set of points from distinct diagonals lies on a sunny line is false. Consequently the solution does not correctly solve the problem."
