            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two given conditions exists are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   be the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \n   \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\).\n\n2. **Existence of \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n   \\]\n\n   are all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n3. **Existence of \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (all non‑sunny).  \n   The only point of \\(T_n\\) not lying on one of these lines is \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Add any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) (for instance \\(y-1=2(x-n)\\)).  \n   The resulting family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and it covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Thus \\(k=1\\) is always attainable.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   Assume a configuration with at least two sunny lines exists.  \n   Let the set of non‑sunny lines be \\(\\mathcal N\\) and the set of sunny lines be \\(\\mathcal S\\) with \\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\ge 2\\).  \n   Remove all lines of \\(\\mathcal N\\); the uncovered points form a (possibly empty) set \\(U\\subseteq T_n\\).  \n   Because each non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, each such line contains an entire *row* (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), an entire *column* (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), or an entire *anti‑diagonal* (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \n   Consequently the complement \\(U\\) is itself a triangular set of the same shape as \\(T_m\\) for some integer \\(m\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le m\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed, after deleting a vertical line \\(x=i\\) the remaining points are exactly those with \\(a\\ge i+1\\); after deleting a horizontal line \\(y=j\\) the remaining points are those with \\(b\\ge j+1\\); after deleting a diagonal \\(x+y=d\\) the remaining points are those with \\(a+b\\le d-1\\). Repeating this operation shows that the uncovered set is a triangle of size \\(m=n-|\\mathcal N|\\).)\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal S\\) must cover all points of \\(U\\).  \n   Hence we are reduced to the following sub‑problem:\n\n   > **Sub‑problem.** For which integers \\(m\\ge 2\\) does there exist a covering of the triangular set \\(T_m\\) by exactly \\(m\\) *sunny* lines?\n\n   We prove that the answer is **never**.\n\n   *Proof of the sub‑problem.*  \n   Suppose \\(m\\ge 2\\) and that \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) are sunny lines covering \\(T_m\\).  \n   For each row \\(y=r\\;(1\\le r\\le m)\\) a sunny line meets the row in at most one point (otherwise the line would be horizontal).  \n   Hence the \\(m\\) lines provide exactly \\(m\\) intersection points with the first row \\(y=1\\); because the first row contains the \\(m\\) points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(m,1)\\), these intersection points must be precisely those \\(m\\) points.  \n   Consequently, after a suitable renumbering we may assume\n\n   \\[\n   L_i\\cap\\{y=1\\}=(i,1)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,m). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Write the equation of \\(L_i\\) in the form \\(y=\\alpha_i x+\\beta_i\\).  \n   From (2) we obtain \\(\\alpha_i i+\\beta_i =1\\), i.e. \\(\\beta_i =1-\\alpha_i i\\).  \n   The intersection of \\(L_i\\) with the second row \\(y=2\\) is therefore at\n\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{2-\\beta_i}{\\alpha_i}= \\frac{2-1+\\alpha_i i}{\\alpha_i}= i+\\frac{1}{\\alpha_i}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the second row contains the points \\((1,2),\\dots ,(m-1,2)\\) and each line meets it in at most one point, the set \\(\\{\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\alpha_i}\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\,\\}\\) must be a subset of \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\).  \n   In particular each \\(\\frac{1}{\\alpha_i}\\) is an integer, say \\(\\frac{1}{\\alpha_i}=t_i\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  \n   Hence \\(\\alpha_i =\\frac{1}{t_i}\\) and (3) becomes \\(x=i+t_i\\).\n\n   Since the line is not vertical, \\(t_i\\neq 0\\).  \n   Moreover the line must not have slope \\(-1\\); this would happen exactly when \\(t_i=-1\\).  \n   Therefore for every \\(i\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   t_i\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The point where \\(L_i\\) meets the third row \\(y=3\\) is obtained by linearity:\n\n   \\[\n   x = i+2t_i . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The third row of \\(T_m\\) consists of the points \\((1,3),\\dots ,(m-2,3)\\).  \n   Hence each value \\(i+2t_i\\) must belong to \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,m-2\\}\\).  \n   Because \\(i\\ge 1\\) and \\(t_i\\ge 1\\) or \\(t_i\\le -2\\) by (4), we have\n\n   \\[\n   i+2t_i\\le i-4\\quad\\text{if }t_i\\le -2,\n   \\qquad\n   i+2t_i\\ge i+2\\quad\\text{if }t_i\\ge 1 .\n   \\]\n\n   In the first case the value is \\(\\le i-4\\le m-4\\); in the second case it is \\(\\ge i+2\\ge 3\\).  \n   Consequently the set \\(\\{i+2t_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\}\\) contains at most \\(m-2\\) distinct integers, whereas the third row contains exactly \\(m-2\\) points.  \n   For the covering to be possible the two sets must coincide, which forces **every** index \\(i\\) to satisfy \\(t_i=1\\).  \n\n   But \\(t_i=1\\) would give slope \\(\\alpha_i=1\\), and then the line through \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,2)\\) has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2-1}{(i+1)-i}=1,\n   \\]\n\n   which is sunny, **except** that the point \\((i+1,2)\\) belongs to the second row only when \\(i+1\\le m-1\\).  \n   For the largest index \\(i=m\\) we would obtain the point \\((m+1,2)\\), which is **not** in \\(T_m\\).  \n   Hence the assignment \\(t_i=1\\) cannot hold for all \\(i\\).  \n\n   This contradiction shows that no covering of \\(T_m\\) by \\(m\\) sunny lines exists when \\(m\\ge 2\\).\n\n   ∎\n\n   Returning to the original problem, (1) tells us that after removing the non‑sunny lines we are left with a triangle \\(T_m\\) with \\(m=n-|\\mathcal N|\\).  \n   If \\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\ge 2\\) then \\(m=n-k\\le n-2\\) and the sub‑problem would require a covering of \\(T_m\\) by exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines, which we have just proved impossible.  \n   Therefore **no configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines can exist** for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only admissible numbers of sunny lines are \\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny) and \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3,\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\text{ and only these values are possible}.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
