cide whether the finite set \\(T_{k}\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nIf a solution of Problem \\(*\\) is known, we obtain a solution of the original problem by adding the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines described above; the added lines are non‑sunny, so the total number of sunny lines remains \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2. The case \\(k=2\\) is impossible\n\n\\(T_{2}\\) consists of the three points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n\\]\n\nAny line that contains two of these points must be parallel either to the \\(x\\)-axis, to the \\(y\\)-axis, or to the line \\(x+y=0\\); all three directions are forbidden for a sunny line.  Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points, and at least three sunny lines are required.  Consequently no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\)\n\n#### 3.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All of them are non‑sunny and together contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n#### 3.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \nAll points with \\(a\\le n-1\\) are covered.  The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  \nThe line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y-1 = 2\\,(x-n)\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(2\\) (hence is sunny) and passes through \\((n,1)\\).  Adding \\(L\\) gives a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly one sunny line.\n\n#### 3.3  \\(k=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_{3}\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nDefine the three lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\; y-1 = (x-1) &&\\text{(slope }1),\\\\\nL_{2}&:\\; y-2 = -\\tfrac12\\,(x-1) &&\\text{(slope }-\\tfrac12),\\\\\nL_{3}&:\\; y-1 = -2\\,(x-2) &&\\text{(slope }-2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA direct verification shows:\n\n* \\(L_{1}\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\);\n* \\(L_{2}\\) contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\);\n* \\(L_{3}\\) contains \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on at least one of the three lines, and all three lines are sunny (their slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), none of which equals \\(0,-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\)).  Adding the remaining \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) yields a configuration for the original problem with exactly three sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Remarks on the general case \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nThe reduction in §1 shows that solving the original problem for all admissible \\(k\\) is equivalent to constructing, for each integer \\(k\\ge 3\\), a family of exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that covers the triangular lattice \\(T_{k}\\).\n\nThe original answer attempted an inductive construction that added two new lines when passing from \\(T_{k}\\) to \\(T_{k+2}\\).  Unfortunately the two lines that were proposed do **not** contain the required border points; the verification in the bug report exhibits concrete counter‑examples (e.g. the point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a>2\\) is not on the claimed line).  Moreover the base case for \\(k=3\\) used a line that fails to pass through \\((2,2)\\), so the induction never starts correctly.\n\nA correct uniform construction for all \\(k\\ge 3\\) would have to respect the following constraints:\n\n* The diagonal \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=k+1\\}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) points, and because any sunny line can meet this diagonal in at most one point (otherwise the line would have slope \\(-1\\)), each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines must be assigned a distinct diagonal point.\n* After fixing the diagonal point for each line, the line may be chosen so that it also passes through additional points of \\(T_{k}\\) (for instance points on the first row or first column).  The collection of all such lines must together cover every point of \\(T_{k}\\).\n\nFinding an explicit formula for the slopes (or a different combinatorial argument guaranteeing the existence of such a family) remains an open sub‑pr