ng \\(b = a+m+i\\) (because \\(b-a=m+i\\)) gives\n   \\[\n   a+m+i = -2a + (2m+2+i)\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; 3a = m+2 .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(a\\) is an integer, (6) guarantees that the unique integer solution of the last equality is\n   \\[\n   a = m+1-i,\\qquad b = i+1 .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(1\\le i\\le m\\), both coordinates are positive and satisfy \\(a+b = (m+1-i)+(i+1)=m+2\\le 2m+2\\); hence the point \\((a,b)\\) indeed belongs to \\(T_k\\) and lies on \\(M_i\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_k\\) belongs either to some \\(L_i\\) (Case 1) or to some \\(M_i\\) (Case 2).  The two families are disjoint, so the total number of lines used is exactly \\(k\\).\n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n   * For \\(k=0\\) we simply take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all are non‑sunny.  \n   * For \\(k=1\\) we use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with any sunny line passing through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   * For every odd \\(k\\ge3\\) we use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non‑sunny) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines described in step 4, after translating them by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\) so that they cover the required translate of \\(T_k\\).\n\n   In each case we obtain \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\n**Conclusion**  \n\nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the non‑negative integers that are either zero or odd.  Hence the answer to the original problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\text{ or }k\\text{ is odd}\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
