of \\(|q|\\).  \n   Inside the triangle \\(T_{k}\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le x\\le k,\\qquad 1\\le y\\le k,\\qquad x+y\\le k+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the total variation of the \\(x\\)–coordinate on the line is at most \\(k-1\\) and the same holds for the \\(y\\)–coordinate.  \n   Moreover, because the line is **not** parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) we have \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) when \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) have the same sign, and \\(|p+q|\\ge 1\\) when they have opposite signs.  \n\n   *If \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) have the same sign* (the line has positive slope), each step along the line increases the sum \\(x+y\\) by \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\).  \n   Since the sum ranges from the minimal value \\(2\\) up to the maximal value \\(k+1\\), the number of lattice points on the line is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) have opposite signs* (the line has negative slope), the sum changes by \\(|p+q|\\ge 1\\).  \n   In this case the variation of the sum is at most \\(k-1\\), so the number of lattice points is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (k-1)+1 = k .\n   \\]\n\n   However, a line with negative slope that stays inside the triangle must also keep both coordinates non‑negative; this forces \\(|p|\\ge 2\\) or \\(|q|\\ge 2\\). Consequently \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) again, and the same bound (1) applies.  \n\n   Thus **any sunny line contains at most**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\displaystyle \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_{k}\\).  The bound is attained by the line of slope \\(1\\) (direction \\((1,1)\\)).\n\n3. **Necessity of oddness.**  \n   The set \\(T_{k}\\) has  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_{k}|=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  If we are to cover it with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality in (4) holds **iff** \\(k\\) is odd; for even \\(k\\ge2\\) the left‑hand side is strictly smaller because  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =k\\cdot\\frac{k}{2}= \\frac{k^{2}}{2}\n   <\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is impossible for even \\(k\\ge2\\).  \n   The cases \\(k=0\\) (empty set) and \\(k=1\\) (the single line \\(y=x\\)) are trivially possible.  \n   Consequently\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{If a covering exists, then }k=0\\text{ or }k\\text{ is odd}.}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for every odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge0\\).  Define the following \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n   *Family A (slope \\(1\\)).*  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(-m\\le i\\le m\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{i}:\\; y = x + i .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   *Family B (slope \\(-2\\)).*  \n   For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le m\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   M_{j}:\\; y = -2x + (2m+2+j) .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The lines in (6) are clearly sunny (slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   The lines in (7) have slope \\(-2\\), also sunny.  \n   All lines are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n   **Claim.**  The family \\(\\mathcal{L}:=\\{L_{i}\\mid -m\\le i\\le m\\}\\cup\\{M_{j}\\mid 1\\le j\\le m\\}\\) covers every point of \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{k}\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\).\n\n   1. **If \\(|b-a|\\le m\\).**  \n      Put \\(i=b-a\\) (an integer with \\(-m\\le i\\le m\\)).  \n      Then \\(b = a+i\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies equation (6) of the line \\(L_{i}\\).  \n      Hence the point lies on a line of Family A.\n\n   2. **If \\(|b-a|> m\\).**  \n      Because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\), the only way to have \\(|b-a|>m\\) is that the larger coordinate equals \\(1\\) and the smaller one is at most \\(m+1\\).  \n      Indeed, assume \\(b-a>m\\). Then \\(b>a+m\\). From \\(a+b\\le 2