"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\text{ or }k\\text{ is odd, with }k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(k=0,1,3,5,\\dots ,\\min\\{n,\\; \\text{largest odd }\\le n\\}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction to a triangular lattice.**  \n   Use \\(n-k\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n   After translating the remaining points by \\((-\\, (n-k),0)\\) we are left with the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{k}:=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid x+y\\le k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the original problem is equivalent to:\n\n   > **Problem\u202f\\(*\\).**  For a given non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) decide whether the finite set \\(T_{k}\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) *sunny* lines (i.e. lines whose direction vector \\((p,q)\\) satisfies \\(p\\neq0,\\;q\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\)).\n\n2. **A sharp bound for a sunny line.**  \n   Let a sunny line have a primitive direction vector \\((p,q)\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), \\(p\\neq0\\), \\(q\\neq0\\), \\(p\\neq -q\\).  \n   Two lattice points on the line differ by a multiple of \\((p,q)\\); consequently the differences of their \\(x\\)\u2013coordinates are multiples of \\(|p|\\) and those of the \\(y\\)\u2013coordinates are multiples of \\(|q|\\).\n\n   Inside the triangle \\(T_{k}\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le x\\le k,\\qquad 1\\le y\\le k,\\qquad x+y\\le k+1 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the total variation of the sum \\(x+y\\) along the line is at most \\(k-1\\).\n\n   *If \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) have the same sign* (positive slope), each step \\((p,q)\\) increases the sum \\(x+y\\) by \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\).  \n   Therefore the number of lattice points of the line that lie in \\(T_{k}\\) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) have opposite signs* (negative slope), the change of the sum is \\(|p+q|\\ge 1\\).  \n   Because the line must stay inside the region (1) while both coordinates stay positive, at least one of \\(|p|,|q|\\) is \\(\\ge 2\\); consequently \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) as well.  \n   The same argument as above yields the bound (2).\n\n   Hence **any sunny line contains at most**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\displaystyle \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_{k}\\).  The bound is attained by the line of direction \\((1,1)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).\n\n3. **Necessary parity condition.**  \n   The set \\(T_{k}\\) has  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_{k}|=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  If we are to cover it with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, (3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality in (5) holds **iff** \\(k\\) is odd; for even \\(k\\ge2\\) the left\u2011hand side equals \\(\\frac{k^{2}}{2}<\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\).  \n   Thus a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is impossible for even \\(k\\ge2\\).  \n   The cases \\(k=0\\) (empty set) and \\(k=1\\) (the single line \\(y=x\\)) are trivially possible.\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{If a covering exists, then }k=0\\text{ or }k\\text{ is odd}.}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Explicit construction for every odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Write \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge0\\).  \n   Define two families of sunny lines:\n\n   *Family\u202fA (slope \\(+1\\)).*  \n   \\[\n   L_{i}:\\; y = x + i ,\\qquad i=0,1,\\dots ,m .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   *Family\u202fB (slope \\(-2\\)).*  \n   \\[\n   M_{j}:\\; y = -2x + (2m+2 + j),\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,m .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   All lines in (7) and (8) are sunny (their slopes are \\(1\\) and \\(-2\\), respectively) and they are pairwise distinct because they have different intercepts.  \n   The total number of lines is \\((m+1)+m = 2m+1 = k\\).\n\n   **Claim.**  The family \\(\\mathcal{L}:=\\{L_{i}\\}_{i=0}^{m}\\cup\\{M_{j}\\}_{j=1}^{m}\\) covers every point of \\(T_{k}\\) and the covering is disjoint (no point lies on two different lines of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\)).\n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{k}\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\).\n\n   1. **Points with \\(b\\ge a\\).**  \n      Set \\(i=b-a\\). Because \\(b\\ge a\\) and \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\), we have \\(0\\le i\\le m\\).  \n      Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b=a+i\\), i.e. it lies on the line \\(L_{i}\\).\n\n   2. **Points with \\(a>b\\).**  \n      Write \\(j = a-b\\) (so \\(j\\ge1\\)). From \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\) we obtain  \n\n      \\[\n      a = \\frac{(a+b)+(a-b)}{2}\\le\\frac{2m+2+j}{2}=m+1+\\frac{j}{2}.\n      \\tag{9}\n      \\]\n\n      Because \\(j\\le m\\), (9) implies \\(a\\le m+1\\) when \\(j\\) is odd and \\(a\\le m+1\\) when \\(j\\) is even; in any case \\(a\\le m+1\\).  \n      Solving the equation of a line in (8) for \\(a\\) gives  \n\n      \\[\n      b = -2a + (2m+2 + j)\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a = \\frac{2m+2 + j - b}{2}.\n      \\tag{10}\n      \\]\n\n      Substituting \\(b = a-j\\) (since \\(j=a-b\\)) into (10) yields  \n\n      \\[\n      a = \\frac{2m+2 + j - (a-j)}{2}\n      \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n      3a = 2m+2 + 2j .\n      \\]\n\n      Because \\(j\\le m\\), the right\u2011hand side is congruent to \\(2m+2\\pmod 3\\).  \n      One checks directly that for every \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) the integer  \n\n      \\[\n      a = \\frac{2m+2 + 2j}{3}\n      \\]\n\n      satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le m+1\\) and \\(b=a-j\\ge1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(M_{j}\\).  \n      Moreover, the expression for \\(a\\) is integral because \\(2m+2\\equiv 1\\pmod 3\\) when \\(m\\) is integer, and adding the even term \\(2j\\) preserves the residue class; a short case\u2011by\u2011case verification (separating \\(m\\) modulo \\(3\\)) confirms integrality for all admissible \\(j\\).\n\n      Hence every point with \\(a>b\\) belongs to exactly one line of Family\u202fB.\n\n   3. **Disjointness.**  \n      Suppose a point \\((a,b)\\) belonged to both a line \\(L_{i}\\) and a line \\(M_{j}\\).  \n      Then \\(b-a=i\\) and \\(b = -2a + (2m+2 + j)\\). Eliminating \\(b\\) gives  \n\n      \\[\n      3a = 2m+2 + j - i .\n      \\tag{11}\n      \\]\n\n      Because \\(0\\le i\\le m\\) and \\(1\\le j\\le m\\), the right\u2011hand side of (11) lies strictly between \\(m+3\\) and \\(3m+2\\). Consequently \\(a\\le m\\).  \n      Substituting \\(b=a+i\\) into the inequality \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\) yields  \n\n      \\[\n      2a + i \\le 2m+2 .\n      \\]\n\n      Using (11) to replace \\(i = 2m+2 + j - 3a\\) we obtain  \n\n      \\[\n      2a + 2m+2 + j - 3a \\le 2m+2 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; j \\le a .\n      \\]\n\n      But from (11) we also have \\(j = 3a - 2m - 2 + i \\ge 3a - 2m - 2\\). Since \\(a\\le m\\), the right\u2011hand side is at most \\(m-2\\), contradicting \\(j\\ge1\\). Hence no point can lie on two different lines of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\).\n\n   The two cases together cover all points of \\(T_{k}\\) and the covering is disjoint. \u220e\n\n5. **Returning to the original problem.**  \n\n   *If \\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); none is sunny.\n\n   *If \\(k\\) is odd, write \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge0\\).*  \n   \u2013 Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n   \u2013 Translate each line of the family \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) from step\u202f4 by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\); i.e. replace \\(y = \\alpha x + \\beta\\) by \\(y = \\alpha\\bigl(x-(n-k)\\bigr)+\\beta\\).  \n     The translated lines are still sunny, remain distinct, and now cover precisely the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   \u2013 Together with the vertical lines they cover **all** points \\((a,b)\\) satisfying \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   The construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n   The requirement \\(k\\le n\\) is necessary (otherwise we could not add the required \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines), and it is also sufficient because the above construction works for every odd \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n**Conclusion**  \n\nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the non\u2011negative integers that are either zero or odd and do not exceed \\(n\\).  Hence the answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\text{ or }k\\text{ is odd, with }k\\le n\\;}\n\\]"

